449
00:45:49,120 --> 00:45:50,320
Here's how it works.

450
00:45:51,340 --> 00:45:55,000
Sound is just air, pushing on air, like ripples in a pond.

451
00:45:55,020 --> 00:45:59,020
Those ripples hit your eardrum and make it shake.

452
00:45:59,020 --> 00:46:02,020
Three tiny bones pass the shake along.

453
00:46:02,020 --> 00:46:05,020
The smallest one is about 3 mm long.

454
00:46:05,020 --> 00:46:09,020
Then comes a snail-shaped tube called the cochlea.

455
00:46:09,020 --> 00:46:14,020
Inside, thousands of tiny hair cells bend like grass in the wind,

456
00:46:14,020 --> 00:46:19,020
and turn every push into a signal for your brain.

457
00:46:19,020 --> 00:46:22,020
Yours bend at almost nothing.

458
00:46:22,020 --> 00:46:24,020
But here's the weird part.

459
00:46:24,020 --> 00:46:28,020
Sound spreads out as it travels, so it gets weaker fast.

460
00:46:28,020 --> 00:46:32,020
Double the distance, and you get a quarter of the sound.

461
00:46:32,020 --> 00:46:38,020
So to hear 10 times farther, your ears need to be 100 times more sensitive.

462
00:46:38,020 --> 00:46:42,020
And here's the catch: your ears got 100 times stronger,

463
00:46:42,020 --> 00:46:44,020
